  "summary": "Cobalt-on-white Russian ceramics from the Gzhel district — boldly brushed flowers on glazed porcelain.",
  "description": "Gzhel's clay fed Moscow's stoves for centuries before its potters turned to majolica and then porcelain, settling by the 1800s into the signature style: a white tin-glazed body painted freehand in cobalt blue, from near-black strokes to silvered washes, with large roses, birds and rhythmic grass borders. The Soviet revival (1930s onward, centered on the Novo-Syolovo factory) made Gzhel a national brand of Russian craft. Every teapot, samovar-shaped decanter and figurine carries the same confident brush — no underdrawing, no corrections.",
